WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:03.550
Let's check in with our caller from the eight
one five area code.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.509
Who's calling today from eight one five.

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:08.189
Hi, this is Jonathan.

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:09.310
I'm from Rockford, Illinois.

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:10.310
Jonathan, uh, hi.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:16.180
Uh, I just had a quick question about on,
so considering the current state of our, like,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.980
political climate, particularly like with
the DNC, you know, trying to rally against

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:25.940
Bernie and obviously the ideological differences
with, uh, from Republicans with Democrats.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.770
Um, would you see like, hypothetically if
Bernie was to become president, do you think

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:34.680
his term would even be successful considering
that there's so many people going against

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.570
them, both on the left and right.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:42.040
Um, and so like his policies are like Medicare
for all, um, you know, universal education,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.040
all that.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.011
Um, what do you mean be successful in trying
to convince other people to sign on to that

00:00:47.011 --> 00:00:48.390
even if he were to be president?

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:52.101
Well, I've taught when I've talked about this
before in the context of health care, I was

00:00:52.101 --> 00:00:56.280
absolutely crushed by a huge portion of the
Bernie supporters in my audience.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.790
And remember, I'm voting for Bernie in the
primary.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:04.470
Um, no, you're absolutely right, which is
that, uh, if it's Bernie versus Trump, Bernie's

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.880
the right choice and we vote for Bernie and
hopefully Bernie wins and he gets as much

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.000
done as possible.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.960
And he will get some things done.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.530
But when you have a significant part of the
democratic Senate, not in favor of Medicare

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.640
for all, when you have the filibuster, when
you have all of these other things, of course

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.200
Bernie is not going to get everything done
that he promises.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.050
And that's no different than any candidate
every, every candidate has.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.700
Trump is going to do a wall.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.770
He was going to do new healthcare, he was
going to eliminate the deficit and eventually

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:36.770
pay off the debt.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.229
Like he's not doing any of that stuff.

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:39.229
Now.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:41.400
Those things were completely unrealistic to
begin with.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.049
But needless to say, it wouldn't be a new
thing that someone is brought into office

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:50.320
and their ambition is bigger than what they
can accomplish.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.670
That is a perfectly realistic and sensible
thing to talk about.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.899
The question though is what can we get done
and making sure that we do it, but you're

00:01:58.899 --> 00:01:59.899
completely right.

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:03.780
Anybody who argues that you're being a defeatist
is being completely unrealistic.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:04.780
Yeah, totally.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:05.780
I agree.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:10.679
Cause you know, I don't think that, you know,
you have to be realistic, especially right

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:11.679
now.

00:02:11.679 --> 00:02:13.319
You know, you gotta take your victories where
you can.

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:14.939
And, um, so I totally agree.

00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:21.290
And I also wanted to ask, um, for, so like,
obviously with the rise of Bloomberg, um,

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.409
and his like, you know, he has obviously he's
been the mayor of New York and he has some

00:02:25.409 --> 00:02:26.459
political experience.

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:32.760
Um, but for someone like Trump or you know,
Tom Stier, um, do you think it's time, and

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.459
I know this probably would never happen, but
that we update like qualifications for the

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:40.549
presidency that involve like maybe having
some type of political experience holding

00:02:40.549 --> 00:02:44.700
some type of office or you know, just maybe
updating what we have already?

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:45.700
Um,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:46.700
no I don't.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.580
So first of all it would be very difficult
to add new requirements, but I actually, I,

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.650
I don't think that political experience should
be required.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:59.189
I would like it if the electorate was more
informed about if you lack political experience,

00:02:59.189 --> 00:03:01.589
what other sort of experience would be a good
replacement.

00:03:01.589 --> 00:03:07.069
I don't think being a reality show host and
using your dad's money to still run a business

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:08.489
that goes bankrupt a bunch of times.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:11.629
Like that doesn't qualify for me, but I wouldn't
want to add more requirements.

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:16.439
The only things I would consider adding are
maybe we should have a president, presidential

00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:21.129
candidates, major party, presidential candidates
undergo a full cognitive assessment.

00:03:21.129 --> 00:03:26.159
And I think that that particularly this presidency
has made a relevant, but no, I wouldn't add

00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:29.180
more requirements about, I don't believe it
would be constitutional.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.180
And, uh, it would be very, very difficult
to achieve.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:33.579
And I don't think it's the right thing either.

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:34.579
Okay.

00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:35.579
All right.

00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:36.579
Fair enough.

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:38.510
All right, well those are all my questions,
so thank you, David.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:39.510
All right, thanks.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.269
I appreciate the phone call very, very much.

00:03:42.269 --> 00:03:43.339
That is going to do it for today.

00:03:43.339 --> 00:03:44.339
I do apologize.

00:03:44.339 --> 00:03:50.059
I wasn't able to get to everyone, but it is
great that there are more people calling in

00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:52.491
than the number that I can get to because
otherwise it would [inaudible]

00:03:52.491 --> 00:03:53.689
be very, very sad.

00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:55.959
We will take calls again.

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:59.519
We will take a quick break and then I will
be back with much more right after this

